        {
          "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bitcoin/bitcoin/git/commits/111a2f779437c8a37fc4cd4eedd027a741163a92",
          "sha": "111a2f779437c8a37fc4cd4eedd027a741163a92",
          "html_url": "https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/commit/111a2f779437c8a37fc4cd4eedd027a741163a92"
        }
      ],
      "message": "fuzz: let FuzzedSock waits report what the real OS can report\n\nFuzzedSock::Wait() and WaitMany() reported either nothing or exactly the\nrequested events. The real OS reports more than that, and the fuzz\ntargets never exercised the callers on it:\n- only some of the requested events, e.g. SendEvent alone on an idle\n  connection;\n- ErrorEvent, with or without RecvEvent, to a socket that requested\n  RecvEvent, as select() does for urgent data;\n- a failed connection, e.g. a reset one: poll() reports it as\n  ErrorEvent whatever was requested, even nothing.\n\nModel these. A failed connection stays failed: waits keep reporting\nit, Recv() returns at most what was buffered before (a bounded amount\npicked when it fails) and then fails for good, and Send() fails. So a\nsocket that did not request RecvEvent can be read from only once its\nconnection failed, and only for a bounded amount, as on the real OS.\n\nWaitMany() now picks the outcome per socket, so that each socket keeps\nits own state. As before, the requested events occur when the fuzz\ninput is exhausted, so that waits do not go on forever.",
      "committer": {
        "name": "Antoine Poinsot",
        "email": "mail@antoinep.com",
        "date": "2026-11-05T19:30:31Z"
      },
